{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSumThicknessBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSumThicknessBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSumClosedForm"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.sq_min_le_sum_hookZ'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.sq_min_le_sum_hookZ'","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HookSumThicknessBound.lean#L148-L152","doc":"The same bound with the index bounds instantiated at the diagram's own dimensions, for a\ndiagram with at least one cell. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.sq_min_le_sum_hookZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.sq_min_le_sum_hookZ","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HookSumThicknessBound.lean#L124-L146","doc":"**Thickness costs hook length.** With `r = min (rowLen 0) (colLen 0)`,\n\nr^2 ≤ Σ_{c ∈ μ.cells} hookZ μ c .\n\nProved from `two_mul_sum_hookZ_eq_sum_sq` by keeping only the `0`-th term of each square sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.card_row_filter_hookZ_le_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.card_row_filter_hookZ_le_one","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HookSumThicknessBound.lean#L82-L104","doc":"**L1.** A row contains at most one cell of any given hook length. This is what bounds the\nhook-length multiplicity `N_m(λ)` by the number of rows, and (via the conjugate statement) by the\nnumber of columns — the input to `R_λ ≤ C · r(λ)` in the REV bound. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.hookZ_strictAnti_row","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.hookZ_strictAnti_row","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HookSumThicknessBound.lean#L70-L80","doc":"Hook lengths are strictly decreasing along a row: `j < j'` gives `h(i,j') < h(i,j)`.\nInduction on the one-step lemma. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.hookZ_succ_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HookSum.hookZ_succ_lt","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HookSumThicknessBound.lean#L59-L68","doc":"Hook lengths strictly decrease along a row: moving one cell to the right drops the arm by\nexactly one and cannot raise the leg, since `colLen` is antitone. Consequently a row contains at\nmost one cell of any given hook length — the combinatorial fact behind `N_m(λ) ≤ ℓ(λ)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}